      "latency_ms": 1978.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 804,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial positive charge on the hydrogens, partial negative charge on the oxygen). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. The transitions between these phases occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature rises, helping regulate climate and body temperature.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires a lot of energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes like sweating.<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between water molecules creates a “skin” that allows insects to walk on water and plants to transport water upward (capillary action).<br>• **Density anomaly** – water is most dense at 4 °C; ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in cold climates. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of it is saltwater in oceans). Freshwater (rivers, lakes, groundwater, glaciers) makes up only ~2.5 % of total water, and most of that is locked in ice or deep underground. |\n| **Biological importance** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and DNA replication all occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Transport** – blood, sap, and cellular cytoplasm are water‑based fluids that move nutrients, waste, and signals.<br>• **Structural role** – water provides turgor pressure in plant cells and contributes to the shape of many organisms. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | • Drinking and cooking<br>• Agriculture (irrigation)<br>• Power generation (hydropower, steam turbines)<br>• Manufacturing (solvent, coolant, cleaning)<br>• Recreation (swimming, boating) |\n| **Environmental role** | • **Climate regulator** – oceans store and redistribute heat via currents.<br>• **Weather formation** – evaporation, condensation, and precipitation drive the water cycle.<br>• **Erosion and sediment transport** – water shapes landscapes over geological time. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n- **Polarity** gives it both hydrogen‑bonding ability and the capacity to dissolve a wide range of substances.\n- **Hydrogen bonds** (weak, transient attractions between the oxygen of one molecule and the hydrogen of another) are responsible for many of its anomalous properties (high boiling point, surface tension, density behavior).\n- **Self‑regulating**: Because of its high heat capacity and latent heat, water buffers temperature changes, making Earth’s climate more stable.\n\n### In a nutshell\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) that can exist as ice, liquid, or vapor. Its ability to form hydrogen bonds gives it extraordinary thermal, mechanical, and solvent properties, which are fundamental to Earth’s climate, geological processes, and all known forms of life."
